A||||3||86SE||EGR Valve busted off - Engine compartment SUPER hot!||||8||
Z||000000||86SE||06-22-2000||06:47 PM||||A couple weeks ago my EGR valve busted off (just the circle part, the base is still bolted on).  I can pull on it and move it around (a piece of metal rod is holding it in) but it doesent look like much hot exhaust gas is escaping.<BR>My question is, with this valve broken the way it is, is it causing the extremely high engine compartment temps??  If you put your hand on the black vents it burns almost instantly!  If you put your hand on the decklid right about the manifolds, it is very hot but not as hot as the vents.<P>Im going to buy a new one anyway, but is this whats causing it to get so hot?<P>PS - Engine does NOT set of any codes!?!?  Weird.......||||reg||8
Z||000001||dman||06-22-2000||07:22 PM||||Hello 86SE.<P>Your EGR valve busted off? Is this a 6 cylinder? If so then what about the EGR tube that leads to the intake plenum? If that is gone too then you'll get a huge vacuum leak...which usually leads to a very high idle as well as red hot glowing manifolds. That may explain the heat. But I'm surprised you didn't get a high idle. You'll definitely want to fix it (one way or another).<P>If your car is a 4 then go ahead and disregard what I said since the 4 doesn't have that tube.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||06-22-2000||07:39 PM||||Its a 6.  The plastic tube is still in one piece/uncracked.  These is no vaccume leak there or no high idle.  Hovers around 900-1100.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||06-23-2000||02:19 AM||||If the EGR tube has a leak in it your exhaust manifolds may be glowing red hot. That may explain the high temps. Correcting the EGR set-up should correct it.||||reg||1
